#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
pandoc -- a stand-in for Pandoc 3.9, the oldest Pandoc TextbookImprover
takes, for running the test suites as a machine with 3.9 would run them,
without 3.9:

    PATH="$PWD/tests/pandoc39:$PATH" bash tests/run-all.sh

It runs the Pandoc installed, 3.11 or later, with 3.9's interface, as read
from 3.9's source, and nothing more:

- `pandoc --version` says 3.9, the installed Pandoc's other lines after
  it.
- A long option 3.9 doesn't have (options-3.9.txt, beside this script:
  --math-method, --completion, and --typst-input came later) stops it as
  3.9 stops, "Unknown option --x." and exit code 6; a unique beginning of
  one it has is taken as that option, as 3.9's getOpt takes it.
- Math is written as 3.9 writes it unless asked for otherwise, as plain
  text (PlainMath, 3.9's default on the command line and in
  WriterOptions; 3.11 made MathML the default), and --mathml, 3.9's way
  to ask for MathML, is passed on as --math-method=mathml, which it means,
  so the installed Pandoc doesn't warn that it's deprecated.
- A Lua filter, reader, or writer has 3.9's functions (data/init.lua, run
  from the --data-dir this gives the installed Pandoc, and given to
  `pandoc lua` as -e before its script): no pandoc.TableBody (3.10) or
  pandoc.types.Sources (3.9.0.1), a pandoc.read that takes a list of
  nothing but Pandoc's own Source objects, PANDOC_VERSION 3.9, and
  pandoc.write and pandoc.WriterOptions writing math as plain text unless
  html_math_method says otherwise.

The Pandoc it runs is the one the environment variable PANDOC39_REAL
names, or else the first on the PATH that isn't a stand-in, this one or
a copy of it, another clone's, say, known by the words this docstring
opens with. It must be 3.11 or later, which has --math-method; `pandoc
--version` stops, saying so, otherwise. run-all.sh, run with this folder
first on the PATH, names that Pandoc in PANDOC39_REAL for every suite,
since a check that hides the PATH's other programs from a run keeps a
link to this and not the Pandoc after it. Each stand-in tells the Pandoc
it runs how many stand-ins deep it is (PANDOC39_DEPTH), and one ten deep
stops, saying so: a stand-in that runs itself, through PANDOC39_REAL or
a script that runs pandoc, stops at once rather than filling the machine
with copies of itself.

What it shows is what the run asks of 3.9's interface, which is where
code written against a newer Pandoc fails on 3.9: under it, the code as
it was before 3.9 was made to work (2026-11-04) failed 781 checks in 12
suites, every conversion stopping on --math-method or pandoc.TableBody.
What it can't show is what 3.9's readers, writers, and templates make of
what they're given: those are the installed Pandoc's. The differences
known are on the installation page.
It's for Linux and macOS, whose PATH finds a script by its name; Windows
doesn't.

